.TH INTRO 1
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to Vectra
.SH DESCRIPTION
Vectra is an operating system written in Odin.
Its structure is Plan 9's.
Every system service is a tree of files behind a message-passing endpoint,
each process has a name space of its own,
and the protocol between a process and a service is 9P2000.L, unmodified.
A user works at a terminal, either the serial line or a window on the
machine's own screen, and types commands at
.IR rc (1).
.SS Name spaces
Almost every object a program can use is a file.
The object a name reaches is decided by a mapping called the
.IR "name space" ,
and every process has one.
A process starts with its parent's name space, shared or copied
(see
.I rfork
in
.IR fork (2)),
and may then change its own without changing anybody else's.
Two processes that hand the kernel the same path may get different files.
.PP
A name space is a tree.
A full file name has the form
.IP
.RI / e1 / e2 /.../ en
.PP
and names a descendant of the root,
which is the first
.BR / .
The tree is assembled from
.IR services ,
each of which serves a tree of its own.
A service is
.I bound
or
.I mounted
onto a name in the name space so far,
and from then on a walk that reaches that name continues in the service's tree.
See
.IR bind (1)
and
.IR bind (2).
.PP
Kernel services have names outside the name space:
a
.B #
followed by one character.
.B #c
is the console device, and it is conventionally bound at
.BR /dev .
Section 3 of this manual describes each one.
A service that a program provides is posted as a name in
.BR /srv ,
and anything that can see the name can mount it.
Section 4 describes the servers that run as programs.
.PP
A directory may be a
.IR "union directory" :
several directories bound to one name, searched in order.
A
.I bind
flag says whether a new member goes at the front or the back of the list,
or replaces it,
and whether files may be created in it.
A name is looked up in each member in turn until it is found,
and a file created in a union goes into the first member
that was bound with creation allowed.
.PP
When a service needs an operation the protocol does not have,
the operation is a file.
A
.B ctl
file takes a line of text, one command per write,
refuses a command it does not know,
and reads back as the commands that would restore its state.
.B /dev/consctl
(see
.IR cons (3))
is the model.
.SS The standard name space
The kernel builds the first name space.
Its root is a small tree of directories served by
.IR root (3),
and the kernel devices are bound into it:
.IP
.EX
#c   /dev     the console, the screen, the keyboard, the mouse; cons(3)
#S   /dev     the disks, as a second member of /dev; sd(3)
#s   /srv     posted services; srv(3)
#e   /env     environment variables; env(3)
#p   /proc    running processes; proc(3)
#b   /bin     the programs the kernel image carries; bin(3)
#l   /lib     the files the kernel image carries; bin(3)
.EE
.PP
The kernel then starts
.IR fatfs (4)
on the boot disk's partition and mounts it at
.BR /n/esp .
The directories
.B /n/esp/vectra/bin
and
.B /n/esp/vectra/lib
are bound before
.B /bin
and
.BR /lib ,
so the programs and files the build staged on the host are what a name
in either reaches.
Then it starts
.IR kfs (4)
on the second disk and mounts it at
.BR /usr .
Home directories live there, and
.B $home
is
.BR /usr/glenda .
There is one user, and no login.
.PP
The last thing the kernel does is run
.B /bin/rc /lib/init
with the console as its three descriptors.
That script, described in
.IR init (8),
starts the keyboard server
.IR kbdfs (4),
the draw server
.IR intuition (4),
a window with a shell in it (see
.IR window (1)),
and finally becomes an interactive
.I rc
on the console.
.B boot complete
is a prompt.
.SS Processes
A process is an address space, a name space, an environment,
and a table of open files.
A program starts with descriptors 0, 1 and 2 open on its console,
its arguments on its stack,
and the name space and environment its parent arranged.
.I Rfork
makes a second process, sharing or copying each of those by a flag,
and
.I exec
replaces the program in place.
.I Spawn
does both in one call.
See
.IR fork (2)
and
.IR exec (2).
.PP
A process ends by
.I exits
with a string, empty for success.
Its parent collects the string with
.IR await .
The string is the exit status
.IR rc (1)
keeps in
.BR $status .
See
.IR wait (2).
.PP
A process is stopped from outside by a
.IR note ,
a string posted to it by its parent, by
.I kill
(see
.IR kill (1)),
or by a write to its
.B /proc
files.
A process that registered a handler with
.I notify
is given the note and may carry on.
One that did not ends with the note as its status.
See
.IR notify (2)
and
.IR proc (3).
.SS Environment
A process's environment is a directory of files, one per variable, at
.BR /env .
.IR Rc (1)
keeps its variables there before it runs a command,
so any program can read
.B /env/path
with
.IR cat (1).
See
.IR env (3).
.PP
The variables set at boot are
.B $path
.RB ( ". /bin" ),
.B $home
.RB ( /usr/glenda ),
.BR $ifs ,
and
.BR $prompt .
.IR Rc (1)
reads them from
.B /lib/rcmain
before its first command.
.SS Using Vectra
The shell is
.IR rc (1),
Plan 9's.
The commands are Plan 9's, with Plan 9's flags where a flag was worth having,
and section 1 lists them.
The line editing of the console is the kernel's:
backspace erases a character,
control-U erases the line,
control-D ends the line without a newline
or, on an empty line, ends the file.
A window (see
.IR window (1))
cooks its own lines and adds control-W and the arrow keys.
.PP
Control-C posts
.B interrupt
to the process group of the shell's current command.
A program that faults ends with a note.
.I Rc
prints the status and prompts again.
.PP
A program is a file in
.BR /bin .
The build stages every program on the boot disk,
so a program is edited on the host,
placed under
.BR build/esp/vectra/bin ,
and runs in the guest without a kernel rebuild.
See
.IR build (8).
.PP
Programs are written in Odin against
.BR sys/libuser ,
described in
.IR intro (2).
A server is a program that makes a pipe,
posts one end in
.BR /srv ,
and answers 9P2000.L on the other.
.IR ramfs (4)
is the smallest complete example.
